readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|Therewith her wrathful courage gan ''appall.''
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. vi. 26.}}
Like the Fire, whose heat doth soon ''appale.''
{{right|''Tofte'', Alba, ii., Pref. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''3.''' To become faint-hearted; lose courage or resolution; become dismayed.—'''4.''' To become weak, flat, stale, and insipid; lose flavor or taste, as fermented liquor.
{{smaller block|I ''appalle'', as drinke dothe or wyne, when it lesith his colour, or ale whan it hath stande longe. {{right|''Palsgrave.''}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To make pale; cause to grow pale; blanch.
{{smaller block|<poem>The answer that ye made to me, my dear, . . .
Hath so ''appalled'' my countenance.</poem>
{{right|''Wyatt'', To his Love.}}}}
'''2†.''' To cause to become weak or to fail; weaken; reduce.
{{smaller block|But it were for an olde ''appalled'' [var. ''palled''] wight.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Shipman’s Tale, l. 102.}}
All other thirst ''appall’d.'' {{right|''Thomson'', Seasons.}}
Severus, being ''appalled'' with age, . . . was constrained to keep his chamber. {{right|''Stow'', Chron., The Romaynes.}}}}
'''3.''' To deprive of courage or strength through fear; cause to shrink with fear; confound with fear; dismay; terrify: as, the sight ''appalled'' the stoutest heart.
{{smaller block|Every noise ''appals'' me. {{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, ii. 2.}}
Does neither rage inflame nor fear ''appal?''
{{right|''Pope'', Imit. of Hor., II. ii. 308.}}}}
'''4†.''' To cause to become weak, flat, or stale, or to lose flavor or taste, as fermented liquor.

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|Wine of its own nature will not congeal and freeze; only it will lose the strength and become ''appalled'' in extremity of cold.
{{right|''Holland'', tr. of Pliny.}}}}

'''appal, appall''' (a̤-pâl′), ''n.'' [< ''appal, appall, v.''] A state of terror; affright; dismay; consternation. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Him [Ajax] viewed the Greeks exulting, with ''appal''<br>The Trojans.
{{right|''Cowper'', Iliad, vi.}}}}

'''Appalachian''' (ap-a̤-lach′i-an or -lā′chi-an), ''a.'' [Named from the ''Appalaches'', an Indian tribe.] Appellative of or pertaining to a system of mountains in eastern North America, extending from Cape Gaspé, in the province of Quebec, to northern Alabama, and divided into many ranges bearing separate names. {{smaller|The whole system has also been called the Alleghanies, after its most extensive division. The name Appalachian was first applied by the Spaniards to the extreme southern part of the system. Also sometimes spelled ''Apalachian'', after the Spanish orthography.—'''Appalachian tea,''' the American name for the leaves of two plants, ''Viburnum cassinoides'' and ''Ilex Cassine'', sometimes used as a substitute for Chinese tea.}}

'''appale†''' (a̤-pāl′), ''v.'' [Early mod. E. also ''apale'', < OF. ''appalir, apalir''; being the same as ''appall'', < OF. ''apallir, appallir'', in closer association with ''pale'': see ''appal, pale''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.'', and ''pall''<sup>2</sup>.] An old spelling of ''appal''.

'''appalement†''', ''n.'' [< ''appale'' + ''-ment''.] An old form of ''appalment''.

'''appall''', ''v.'' and ''n.'' See ''appal''.

'''appalling''' (a̤-pâ′ling), ''p. a.'' Causing or fitted to cause dismay or horror: as, an ''appalling'' accident; an ''appalling'' sight.
{{smaller block|All the avenues of enquiry were painted with images of ''appalling'' suffering, and of malicious dæmons.
{{right|''Lecky'', Europ. Morals, II. 243.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''appallingly''' (a̤-pâ′ling-li), ''adv.'' In a manner to appal or transfix with fright; shockingly.

'''appalment''' (a̤-pâl′ment), ''n.'' [< ''appal'' + ''-ment.''] The state of being appalled; depression occasioned by fear; discouragement through fear. Also spelled ''appallment,'' and formerly ''appalement.'' [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The furious slaughter of them was a great discouragement and ''appalement'' to the rest. ''Bacon,'' Hen. VII., p. 35.}}

'''appanage''' (ap′a̤-nāj), ''n.'' [< F. ''appanage, appenage'' (Cotgrave), ''apanage,'' now only ''apanage'' (> E. also ''apanage''), < OF. ''apaner'' = Pr. ''apanar,'' < ML. *''appanare, apanare,'' furnish with bread, < L. ''ad,'' to, + ''panis'' (> F. ''pain''), bread.] '''1.''' Originally, in the feudal law of France, that which was granted to the sons of the sovereign for their support, as lands and privileges, and which reverted to the crown on the failure of male heirs.
{{smaller block|In Scotland, at a later date, appanage was the patrimony of the king’s eldest son, upon whose death or succession to the throne it reverted to the crown. In England, the duchy of Cornwall is sometimes regarded as an appanage of the Prince of Wales; in addition, he and other members of the royal family receive from Parliament allowances amounting to £156,000 out of the annual income derived from the hereditary crown lands surrendered to Parliament in the time of William IV.}}
{{smaller block|France could little afford to see Normandy separated from its body, even though it was to form an ''apanage'' of one of its own princes.
{{right|''E. A. Freeman,'' Norm. Conq., III. 78.}}}}
'''2.''' Whatever belongs or falls to one from one’s rank or station in life.
{{smaller block|“I prefer respect to admiration,” said Flora; “but I fear that respect is not the ''appanage'' of such as I am.”
{{right|''Disraeli,'' Coningsby, iv. 8.}}}}
